-- title:
--   Integrality over K[j] transports along K-algebra maps
-- statement:
--   Let $K$, $F$ and $F'$ be commutative rings with $F$ and $F'$ both $K$-algebras, and let $\varphi \colon F \to F'$ be a homomorphism of $K$-algebras. Let $j, x \in F$, and suppose that $x$ is integral over the $K$-subalgebra $\mathrm{Adjoin}_K\{j\} \subseteq F$ generated by $j$, that is, $x$ satisfies a monic polynomial with coefficients in that subalgebra. The conclusion is that $\varphi(x) \in F'$ is integral over the $K$-subalgebra $\mathrm{Adjoin}_K\{\varphi j\} \subseteq F'$ generated by $\varphi(j)$. Integrality here is `IsIntegral` of an element of $F$ (respectively $F'$) over the corresponding subalgebra regarded as a base ring, the ring structure on the target being the one induced by the ambient algebra.
--
--   This is the routine transport of the relation 'integral over $K[j]$' along a $K$-algebra homomorphism; in the formalisation it moves integrality over $\mathbb{Q}[j]$ from one coefficient ring of formal/Laurent series to another, for instance along a coefficient embedding $\mathbb{Q}((q)) \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$. It is used in the treatment of modular units, cuspidality and modular forms mod $p$, where elements are recognised as integral over the $j$-line after a change of coefficients.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_isIntegral_adjoin_map_algHom.lean

